## ✏️ A. Tone & Language Adjustments

Let’s look at small shifts that can reduce pressure while keeping your message clear:

| **Moment**          | **Original Phrase**                                     | **Try Instead**                                                                   | **Why This Helps**                            |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| At the start        | “We’re already behind. Let’s do number 5 quickly.”      | “Let’s just take this one step at a time—we’ll get through it together.”          | Reduces time pressure, signals partnership    |
| When stuck          | “If you were trying, we wouldn’t still be on number 5.” | “It looks like you’re stuck—want to walk me through what you’re thinking?”        | Supports autonomy and protects self-worth     |
| When child is upset | “Stop crying and just focus.”                           | “You seem really overwhelmed. Let’s take a breath together before we keep going.” | Validates emotion and gives room to reset     |
| On mistakes         | “You know that’s not right. Think for half a second.”   | “Hmm, not quite—let’s slow it down and think through it again.”                   | Keeps tone neutral and encourages persistence |
| On resistance       | “You haven’t earned a break.”                           | “Want to try two more and then take a quick break?”                               | Builds cooperation through shared goals       |

Also, when your child said *“I don’t like homework with you,”* it was a bid for emotional safety, not defiance. A warm repair like *“I hear that. Let’s see if we can make it feel better next time”* could begin rebuilding connection in the moment.

